["er.record/1",{"commit":{"envelope":{"actor":"human:timo","causation":"cmd-child-20747975e013c5222c52e3104aafbba120505cde931280f48d4e8ee092aa3b3c","correlation":"protocol-artifact-new","record":{"changed":{"document":{"events":[],"fields":{"intent":{"causation":null,"command_id":"cmd-child-20747975e013c5222c52e3104aafbba120505cde931280f48d4e8ee092aa3b3c","command_type":"aep.entity.create/v1","correlation_id":"protocol-artifact-new","execution_id":null,"expected_revision":null,"payload":{"command":"create-entity","data":{"body":"## Context\n\nToday a writer finds an entity by its name, so one real-world entity can end up as two nodes of different types with the same name. AGENTS.md invariant 3: names are not identities. The resolver (A10, design § 45) takes a typed reference and answers against one canonical snapshot, deterministically, with no fuzzy or similarity matching.\n\nThe rule, as far as it is decided:\n\n- A candidate is a node of the canonical root whose `type_id` equals the reference `type_id` exactly and whose `aliases` contain one of the reference aliases, compared byte for byte. `canonical_name` is never compared.\n- One candidate: `Resolved`. None: `ProposeNew`, carrying the type and aliases; nothing is written, and the caller mints the id (`ekr mint node`). More than one: `Ambiguous`, listing every candidate in id order. The resolver never picks one.\n- Refused, not guessed: a reference with no alias (`reference-without-identity`); a reference carrying identifying keys (behind `decision-blocker:typed-reference-identifying-keys`); a reference whose type is abstract or has declared descendants (`reference-type-has-subtypes`, behind `decision-blocker:typed-reference-subtype-matching`).\n\nThe resolver is a pure function over `ekr-graph` canonical state and the `ekr-ontology` schema. It reads; it holds no writer (invariant 1).\n\n## Design sections\n\n§ 10, § 45, § 63 step 3, § 74.4; A10 (`docs/predecessors.md` § 2).\n\n## Domain relations\n\n- `Node → NodeType`, many-to-one, a reference. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.Node` relation `type`, cardinality one, via `type_id`). This is why a match compares exactly one `type_id`.\n- `GraphRoot → Node`, one-to-many, the root owns its nodes. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.GraphRoot` relation `nodes`, `owns`, via `root_id`). Candidates come from the canonical root only.\n- `Node → alias`, one-to-many, the node owns its aliases, and nothing stops two nodes holding the same alias. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.Node` field `aliases: List<String>`; this is a field, not a `relations:` entry, and no `ess/1` declaration makes an alias unique). That is why `Ambiguous` exists.\n- `NodeType → parent NodeType`, many-to-many. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/ontology.yaml`, `ekr.ontology.NodeType` fields `parents: List<TypeId>` and `abstract_type`). Used here only to detect and refuse a type with descendants.\n\n## Tests the story ships\n\n- Two nodes with the same `canonical_name` and different types: a reference to either type resolves to that type node only.\n- A property test: permuting the snapshot node order or the reference alias order gives byte-identical outcomes.\n- An alias differing by case or whitespace does not match.\n- The crate declares no string-similarity dependency, held by a test that reads its manifest.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `crates/ekr-integrate/` (new crate: `Cargo.toml`, `src/lib.rs`, `src/resolve.rs`, `tests/resolve.rs`)\n- `Cargo.toml` (workspace): the one `members` line\n\n## Acceptance\n\nOver one canonical snapshot, a typed reference resolves to the single node of exactly its type that holds one of its aliases, to `ProposeNew` when no node does, and to `Ambiguous` listing every candidate when more than one does, and two nodes sharing a name but differing in type never resolve to each other.\n","status":"draft","title":"Resolve a typed reference by alias to one node, a new-node proposal